— and I did not merely re-run its command, I re-asked its question over the FULL corpus: `16cb53ad5..HEAD` returns `rotated: false`, ZERO moving commits, so the banked `d47f32f68edb…` is CURRENT and now established more robustly than before, still without a test run.** 🎯 **What earns it a row is the DIRECTION and the MOMENT: the predicate was landed one fire ago as a labour-saving cure, it fails toward TRUSTING a figure that has already moved, and it is structurally blind on precisely the fires that drain code — the fires most likely to consult it.** 🛠️ **CURE: `node scripts/engine-hash-staleness.mjs <banked-commit> [<until-commit>] [--strict] [--json]`, which DERIVES its pathspec from `ENGINE_SOURCE_INPUTS` rather than transcribing it (the F-2207-1 inversion — a prose list of eleven paths is a subject set that rots silently the day the corpus grows a twelfth, and an imported one cannot).** It declares its corpus on stdout ALWAYS including the happy path (F-2208-1), is anchored to `import.meta.url` because `git log -- <pathspec>` resolves against the PROCESS cwd while reporting repo-relative names (F-2221-1/F-2220-1 — a cwd-rooted corpus narrows to nothing from any subdirectory and prints an affirmative "no rotation"), and REFUSES an empty corpus rather than reporting an unearned CURRENT (F-2217-1). 🚦 **THE 2-vs-1 EXIT SPLIT IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN DECORATIVE: this file imports its corpus from another module, and an uncaught import throw exits 1 — which in this protocol MEANS "ROTATED". A failure wearing a verdict's exit code is F-2212-1 exactly, so every failure path is caught and mapped to 2 = "could not answer".** 🔬 **CONTROLS, each proven by running rather than by reading: REVERSE CONTROL — window `5b85f8583..b80253bff`, where the one-file predicate returns EMPTY (false-current), the derived tool reads **`⚠️ ROTATED`, rc=1**, naming the moving commit · CWD-INVARIANCE — run from `scripts/`, byte-identical stdout · REFUSAL ARMS — unresolvable ref and missing argument both `⛔ CANNOT ANSWER` at rc=2, with the refusal on STDOUT (F-2211-1: an stdout-classifying caller reads an empty string as silence).** 🚫 **DELIBERATELY NOT ROOTED IN ANY npm BATTERY, AND THE RESTRAINT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN LAZY — this is the sharpest constraint in the finding: adding a `test:ledger-guards` leg edits `package.json`, which IS in `ENGINE_SOURCE_INPUTS`, so the guard would ROTATE THE VERY HASH ITS TOOL REPORTS ON and add a further movement to the corpus the owner is being asked to rule on. A guard here would corrupt its own subject.** `desk-state-audit.mjs` is unrooted for the sibling reason (F-1566-2); this is a triage READ like §2.0c's runner check, not a gate. ⓘ **This fire likewise wrote no battery leg, so it adds no movement of its own — the row cannot move the number it reports.** 📐 **AND IT PRICES THE OWNER'S OPEN FORK WITH A NUMBER RATHER THAN AN IMPRESSION: `package.json` alone drives **43 of 54 (79.6%)** of ALL engine-identity rotations in the window. That is direct evidence for F-2416-2(ii) — *should `package.json` be in the identity corpus at all, when F-1300-4 REQUIRES every ledger-writing fire to append its guard to that very file?* — and it says the false-positive engine is four-fifths of the whole signal.** 🚩 **DESK-NOT-OWED: evidence for the OPEN F-2416-2(ii) / F-2297-1 fork, exactly as F-2437-1 and F-2438-1 were. It asks for no new word and adds no item; desking it would split one owner question across four rows and make the fork look bigger than it is.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is the rung under F-2358-1's own lesson: that finding said *enumerate every key your cure selects on*. This one says CHECK THE SELECTOR AGAINST THE CORPUS ITS CLAIM COVERS. A predicate inherited from a predecessor is right about the mechanism its author SAW and silent about the ones they did not — F-2438-1 was reasoning about the recurring `package.json` false positive, and generalised a claim about ONE driver into a test for the WHOLE hash. When a shortcut replaces an expensive measurement, ask what the expensive measurement READS, and make the shortcut read the same thing.**
