d **main**, which is F-2409-1's original scoping (*"invisible to anything riding the deploy and bites everything riding main"*) — restored, not newly broken. **The live cost today is a red drain-gate battery, not a dead door.** ➡️ **What it needs is the ruling already on the desk, not a fire's patch.**
252:🧩 **F-2426-1 — s2425’S DECLARED BOUNDARY MEASURED AND DISCHARGED: THE INDIRECT-REACH HOLE IS EMPTY TODAY, FORWARD-LIVE, AND DELIBERATELY NOT WIDENED (s2426, 2026-09-01; A NEGATIVE RESULT BANKED WITH ITS MECHANISM).** s2425 cured the marker census’s THIRD conjunct and closed its row with a declared boundary naming the FIRST: *“a guard reaching playwright INDIRECTLY — `npm run test:e2e -- --list` names no `playwright` literal … is a genuine hole and is the next place I would look.”* **I looked. It is EMPTY.** 📊 **RE-MEASURED over the same 174 `scripts/*.test.mjs`: `6` carry the list flag, and the ONLY one lacking the `playwright` literal is `worker-type-coverage.test.mjs` — the `tsc --listFiles` file s2425 had ALREADY classified and correctly excluded. ZERO live offenders; no collection guard reaches playwright indirectly today.** ⚠️ **EMPTY IS NOT IMPOSSIBLE, AND THAT IS THE HALF WORTH CARRYING (F-2214-1: record the MECHANISM, not the verdict, so the next fire inherits a reason): the idiom is ordinary house style here, so the boundary is FORWARD-LIVE rather than hypothetical — `12` of the 174 test files already spawn `npm`, and `7` `package.json` scripts wrap playwright (`test`, `test:preview`, `test:release`, `test:release-base`, `test:asset-diet`, `census`, `verify:visual`). A future collection guard written as `npm run test:preview -- --list` satisfies conjuncts 2 and 3 and is invisible to conjunct 1 — silently, in the one direction that file exists to make loud.** 🚫 **DELIBERATELY NOT WIDENED, on s2425’s OWN `fork` reasoning rather than on inertia, and MEASURED rather than assumed: the intersection of “spawns npm” and “carries the list flag” is exactly ONE file and it is SELF, already exempt by name — so widening buys NO detection today, while `npm` is common enough in this corpus to eventually meet the other two conjuncts and accuse an honest file: the noise that gets a guard excused into uselessness (F-1460-1).** ➡️ **THE NUMBER TO WATCH IS NAMED AT THE SITE so this cannot decay into prose: whether any npm-spawning test file ever gains the list flag. On the day that intersection exceeds SELF, re-measure and widen; until then a red would fire only on honest work.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY: NOT A DEFECT AND NOT A CURE — stated plainly so it is not over-read. Nothing was broken, no verdict moves, and the guard is green 9/9 before and after a COMMENT-ONLY edit. What changed is that a hole recorded as UNMEASURED is now measured, with its expressibility priced, so no future fire spends a budget re-deriving it.** 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF: this streak has spent six fires learning to *enumerate the keys a selector uses*; the rung above it is that A DECLARED BOUNDARY IS AN UNCLAIMED MEASUREMENT (F-2219-1) — and the cheapest one on any board, because its author has already done the hard part of naming exactly where to look. **Take your predecessor’s declared limit before you take their recommended cure: the limit costs minutes and either retires an aim or hands you the next finding.** Here it retired one.** Evidence: `a66c712f4` (comment amendment, `scripts/collection-guards-subject-set-guard.test.mjs`). Guard green 9/9, 0.51 s, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`.
269:✅ **F-2418-2 — DISCHARGED s2419 (2026-09-01), landed deliberately as its author asked. The ceiling is on `test:ledger-guards`; see F-2419-1 below for the execution evidence. Original row preserved verbatim:  **F-2418-2 — F-1400-4's HANG CEILING WAS APPLIED TO ONE BATTERY AND NOT ITS SIBLING, SO THE MANDATED LAST ACT OF EVERY LEDGER-WRITING FIRE IS UNBOUNDED — AND IT HUNG FOR 11.9 MINUTES OF MY WINDOW (s2418, 2026-09-01). PRICED, NOT BUILT — bounded, fire-authorable, one line.** F-1400-4 is explicit about the harm: *"`node --test` defaults to NO test timeout, so ONE non-terminating sim hung this whole battery with no bound, no diagnostic and no partial report … an unattended fire that hits that burns its entire window looking merely slow."* Its cure landed in `scripts/run-node-guards.mjs` as `--test-timeout=300000`, with a long comment arguing the number. 🕳️ **BUT `test:ledger-guards` DOES NOT GO THROUGH THAT RUNNER — `package.json` invokes `node --test <98 files>` BARE, with no `--test-timeout` — so the cheap battery, the one F-1300-4 makes the MANDATED LAST ACT of every fire that writes a ledger row, has no ceiling at all.** 📊 **MEASURED THIS FIRE ACROSS THREE RUNS OF THE SAME TREE, and the spread IS the finding: run 1 → 953 tests, 952 pass, **1 fail** (a cross-test race, below) · run 2 → **HUNG**, `law-pointer-guard.test.mjs` at **715,920 ms** and still blocked at **0.0% CPU** when I killed the chain, 931/932 otherwise passing · run 3, the same 98 files with an explicit `--test-timeout=120000` → **rc=0, 953/953, 0 fail, 33.3 s**.** ✅ **THE TREE IS GREEN — that is established by run 3 and by isolation, not assumed: `law-pointer-guard.test.mjs` alone is **22/22 in 1.9 s**, and the whole leg completes in 33 s. Both anomalies are ARRANGEMENT, not content.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: nothing is broken, no verdict was wrong, and the battery is green on demand. The cost is a fire's WINDOW — 11.9 minutes of mine — and it is paid at the worst possible moment, because this battery runs LAST, when a fire has least time left.** 🎯 **What earns it a row is that F-1400-4 already priced this exact harm, already built the exact cure, and the sibling battery never adopted it — this factory's most-repeated shape (F-2153-1 · F-2204-1 · F-2350-1 · F-2360-1 · F-2365-1 · F-2403-1 · F-2414-1), here between two legs of the same `package.json`.** 🛠️ **THE CURE IS ~20 CHARACTERS: add `--test-timeout=300000` to the `test:ledger-guards` node leg, matching the sibling.** ⚠️ **AND IT IS NOT THE FORBIDDEN "RAISE IT UNTIL IT GOES GREEN" (F-1410-2): there is no bound here to raise — this ADDS a ceiling where none exists, which is strictly tighter than the status quo in every case.** The number needs no new measurement to justify: the ENTIRE 98-file leg runs in **33.3 s**, so a 300 s PER-TEST ceiling is ~9× the whole battery and can only ever catch a hang, never a slow-but-working test — precisely F-1400-4's own argument for the same figure. 🚫 **DELIBERATELY NOT BUILT THIS FIRE, and the reason is a law rather than caution: F-1384-1 — a cure written in the fire that invents it has not been executed once — and this fire has already landed one cure with its guard (F-2418-1). It is also a `package.json` edit, which moves the engine identity hash while that corpus question sits unruled on the owner's desk (F-2416-2), so it should land deliberately rather than as a rider.** 🐞 **A SECOND, SEPARABLE DEFECT FOUND IN THE SAME BATTERY AND DIAGNOSED IN FULL — A CROSS-TEST RACE OVER THE REAL `scripts/` DIRECTORY.** Run 1's single failure was `desk-birth-gateless-row-guard.test.mjs:138`, `ENOENT: copyfile … scripts/tmp-s2390-shadow-28155-413266313.mjs`. **Neither guard is wrong on its own.** `desk-birth-gateless-row-guard.test.mjs:201–205` snapshots the fixture by `readdirSync(HERE)` then `copyFileSync` each non-test `*.mjs`; `drain-block-queue-nearmiss-guard.test.mjs:59–66` writes `tmp-s2390-shadow-${process.pid}-${hash}.mjs` **into that same real directory** and removes it in a `finally` — and its comment at `:56–57` explains why it MUST live there (*"a /tmp copy can silently never run (F-2215-1's trap), which looks exactly like the silence under test"*). `drain-block-subject-divergence-guard.test.mjs:47` does the same with `tmp-s2262-shadow-*`. **`node --test` runs these files concurrently, so a shadow that appears between the `readdir` and the `copy` is an ENOENT.** ➡️ **REC: make the snapshot tolerate a file that vanishes mid-copy (skip `ENOENT`). That cannot mask a genuinely missing file, because the fixture's real check is downstream — `:206` spawns `desk-birth-guard.mjs` inside the fixture, which fails loudly if anything it needs is absent.** ⓘ **Observed once in three runs; the other two runs did not reproduce it, so it is intermittent and neither figure should be read as a rate.** 💡 **REUSABLE: when a battery's cure is a RUNNER flag, it protects only the batteries that go through that runner — so ask which sibling invokes the tool BARE. And a test that must write into a shared real directory is a concurrency hazard to every other test that ENUMERATES that directory; the writer was careful and correct, and the collision still happened, because neither file can see the other.** Evidence: `package.json` → `test:ledger-guards` (bare `node --test`, no timeout) vs `scripts/run-node-guards.mjs` (`--test-timeout=300000` + its rationale comment), `scripts/desk-birth-gateless-row-guard.test.mjs:138,201–206`, `scripts/drain-block-queue-nearmiss-guard.test.mjs:56–66`, `scripts/drain-block-subject-divergence-guard.test.mjs:47`, three run transcripts this fire. Prior: F-1400-4, F-1410-2, F-1384-1, F-2215-1, F-1300-4.
271:🔒 **F-2419-1 — s2418's PRICED HANG CEILING IS BUILT: `test:ledger-guards` NOW CARRIES `--test-timeout=300000`, AND THE FLAG WAS PROVEN VERDICT-NEUTRAL BEFORE IT LANDED (s2419, 2026-09-01).** F-2418-2 measured the harm and declined to build it, for a stated reason rather than caution: F-1384-1 — *a cure written in the fire that invents it has not been executed once* — plus a request that it *"land deliberately rather than as a rider"*. **Both conditions are satisfied by a successor fire landing it alone, which is what this row records.** ✅ **THE INHERITED PREMISE WAS RE-VERIFIED BY READING, NOT INHERITED (Mistake #4): `package.json` invoked `test:ledger-guards` as a BARE `node --test <98 files>` with no ceiling, while its sibling `test:node-guards` routes through `scripts/run-node-guards.mjs`, which appends `--test-timeout=${NODE_GUARDS_TEST_TIMEOUT_MS}` at `:71` from `NODE_GUARDS_TEST_TIMEOUT_MS = 300_000` at `:36`.** Both halves confirmed at the code, not from the finding's prose. 📊 **PROVEN VERDICT-NEUTRAL BEFORE THE EDIT, WHICH IS THE ONLY REASON IT COULD LAND AS A ONE-LINER: the same 98 files run WITH `--test-timeout=300000` → **rc=0, 953 tests, 953 pass, 0 fail, 0 cancelled, 0 skipped, 71.1 s**. The flag moves no verdict.** ⓘ **AND THE NUMBER NEEDS NO NEW ARGUMENT: the ENTIRE 98-file leg completes in 71.1 s, so a 300 s PER-TEST ceiling is ~4x the whole battery — it can only ever catch a hang, never a slow-but-working test, which is verbatim the sibling's own rationale.** ⚖️ **THIS IS NOT THE FORBIDDEN "RAISE IT UNTIL IT GOES GREEN" (F-1410-2), and the direction is what makes that certain: there was no bound here to raise. The change is unbounded -> bounded, strictly tighter in every case.** 🛡️ **THE 13 FILES IN THIS BATTERY THAT DECLARE THEIR OWN PER-TEST BUDGET ARE UNTOUCHED — a per-test `{ timeout }` overrides the CLI default (proven by execution s1479, and re-attested here by the 0-cancelled result across all 953).** 🔎 **THE ONE REAL RISK WAS CHECKED BEFORE THE EDIT RATHER THAN AFTER, AND IT IS THE TRANSFERABLE HALF: `battery-manifest.mjs` DERIVES its roster from this very string, so a malformed flag could have silently changed which files the manifest believes are in the battery.** It extracts with `/\b((?:scripts|src)\/[^\s'"`]+\.test\.mjs)\b/g` — a FILE-PATH pattern — so a flag token cannot enter the roster. **Measured before and after: 98 files extracted both times.** 💡 **REUSABLE: when you add a token to a command string, ask who PARSES that string, not merely who RUNS it. A battery's command line is a corpus for somebody.** ⓘ **ENGINE-HASH DISCLOSURE, stated plainly because the owner has a live unruled fork on it (F-2416-2): `package.json` is in `ENGINE_SOURCE_INPUTS`, so this edit MOVES the engine identity hash — and it is once again exactly the class F-2417-1 measured at 70%, an edit our own law mandates. **It changed no verdict: `engine-era-guard` was RED on main BEFORE this fire (measured at entry, actual `e8fda0d3...` vs pinned `c0a015ae...`) and is red after, for the same reason.** No green became red.** Evidence: `package.json` -> `test:ledger-guards` (now `node --test --test-timeout=300000 ...`), `scripts/run-node-guards.mjs:36,71` (the sibling constant it matches), a 953/953 pre-edit control run, a roster-extraction control (98 before, 98 after). Prior: F-2418-2, F-1400-4, F-1410-2, F-1384-1, F-1300-4, F-2417-1.
278:🕳️ **F-2418-1 — THE PER-LEG GATE PRIOR CONTAINS ZERO REAL GATE RUNS: ALL 465 ROWS ARE ITS OWN TEST'S FIXTURE OUTPUT, AND IT REPORTS THE ONE BATTERY THAT IS RED ON MAIN AS 100% GREEN (s2418, 2026-09-01; MEASURED AND CURED, guard rooted).** F-2309-1 asked for a *"cheap prior"* so a fire meeting a node-guard red would not have to pay for a control run every time; F-2312-1 ⑦(b) named the cure — *"`run-guards.mjs` appends its existing `rows` to a JSONL after the loop"* — and it LANDED, as `logs/guard-stats.jsonl`. **It has been collecting since 2026-08-25 and NOTHING HAS EVER READ IT** (measured over the corpus: the only mentions are its writer `run-guards.mjs`, its own two guards, a `lane-runner-v3.sh` comment, and F-2398-2's clause in `scripts/fire.md` — no instrument consumes it). 📊 **SO THE FIRST FIRE TO READ IT WAS THIS ONE, AND EVERY ROW IN IT IS FICTION: 465 rows · 105 runIds · `seconds: 0` on ALL 465 · and it reports `test:node-guards` as **64 runs / 0 red / 100% GREEN** on a day `engine-era-guard` — that battery's FIRST leg — reds on main at rc=1 (re-derived this fire: `417ac150…c3c864` vs pinned `c0a015ae…`).** ⚙️ **THE TELL IS ARITHMETIC, NOT INTUITION, AND IT IS THE ONLY REASON THIS WAS CAUGHT: `test:node-guards` takes ~530 s (F-2166-2's floor), so a `seconds: 0` row for it is not unlikely, it is IMPOSSIBLE.** The rows are otherwise perfectly authentic-looking — they carry REAL short HEAD shas, every one a genuine fire lock commit (`b349ace6e`, `1d41d4763`, … `e95229211`), which is exactly what makes the corpus read as a real history. 🔑 **MECHANISM: `run-guards.mjs:45-46` anchors `statsPath` to the SCRIPT TREE, not to cwd** — `repoRoot = path.dirname(path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)))` — **which is CORRECT for the gate (it is F-2220-1's anchoring lesson applied properly) and is precisely what makes a TEST dangerous.** `scripts/run-guards.test.mjs:9` spawns the **REAL** script (`new URL('./run-guards.mjs', import.meta.url)`, not a copy) from stub `package.json` fixtures in `os.tmpdir()`, and had **ZERO** `GR_GUARD_STATS_PATH` mentions — so `cwd` was the tmpdir while the stats file resolved back to the production corpus, and the stub legs (`node -e ""`) returned instantly, which is where `seconds: 0` comes from. ✅ **PROVEN BY MANUFACTURING, NOT BY READING (control asserted its own validity first, F-2215-1): recorded 407 rows → ran `node --test scripts/run-guards.test.mjs` (12/12 pass, 8.0 s) → **465 rows, DELTA +58**, among them 8 fictional `test:node-guards` rows reading `rc=0`, one `test:stats` reading `rc=3` and one `signal:SIGKILL` (this file's manufactured failure arms), every one stamped with **my own lock commit `9656073c7`** as its head.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED — THIS IS NOT A FALSE GREEN IN ANY GATE AND NOTHING IS BROKEN: `run-guards.test.mjs` passes 12/12 correctly both before and after, the persistence write sits inside a `try` that provably cannot change a guard exit (its sibling guard asserts exactly that), and the REALISED COST IS ZERO because nothing reads the file.** 🎯 **What earns it a row is the DIRECTION AND THE TIMING: the corpus was built to be READ, it is the designated cheap prior for exactly the situation the board is in RIGHT NOW (a red `test:node-guards`), and it is pre-poisoned before its first reader ever arrives — a reader who would be told, affirmatively and with real commit shas, that the failing battery has never failed.** That is the inverse of this factory's most-repeated finding: not a cure with no reader, but a cure whose DATA went fictional while it waited for one. 🛠️ **CURE: `run-guards.test.mjs` now pins `GR_GUARD_STATS_PATH` inside its own per-test fixture dir at BOTH spawn sites (the `run()` helper and the `withShim` arm), with the reason stated at the site. The sibling `guard-stats-persistence.test.mjs` has ALWAYS done this — the practice, the env var and the precedent all existed and this one file never adopted them (F-2398-2's own fire redirected to a temp file and wrote *"live corpus untouched"*).** ✅ **CURE PROVEN, AND THE VACUOUS READING RULED OUT: post-cure the live file grew by **+0** across a full run while the arms still pass 12/12 — and because "0" is also what a SILENTLY BROKEN persistence path looks like, a separate control confirmed the rows are still written, just elsewhere (`guard-stats: appended 1 record(s) to <tmp>/redirected.jsonl`, rc=0).** 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/guard-stats-test-isolation-guard.test.mjs` — 4 arms, 48 ms, rooted in `test:ledger-guards` (98 node legs, was 97). The subject set is DERIVED from the resolution idiom, never transcribed, so a new spawner is covered the day it lands; an EMPTY subject set REFUSES rather than passing vacuously (F-2217-1).** 🔬 **TEETH — six variants manufactured on scratch copies, control green, and the sweep doubled as a reachability audit (s2226's duty): EVERY arm reds under at least one variant, so none is decoration.** Pre-cure spawner restored → arm 1 · redirect aimed back at `logs/guard-stats.jsonl` (the defect wearing the cure's clothes) → arm 2 · subject set emptied → arms 1+2 REFUSE · runner drops `GR_GUARD_STATS_PATH` → arm 4 · runner stops anchoring to the script tree → arm 4 · selector widened back to the loose form → **the over-reach control, arm 3, ALONE**. ⚠️ **ONE FALSE POSITIVE PAID FOR IN THIS FIRE AND IT IS THE TRANSFERABLE HALF: my first selector was *"mentions `run-guards.mjs` anywhere AND spawns anything"*, and it reddened on `gate-caller-audit.test.mjs` — which spawns `gate-caller-audit.mjs` and merely WRITES a synthetic stub named `scripts/run-guards.mjs` into its own fixture repos.** A name in fixture CONTENT is not a spawn of the real thing. **The over-reach control now pins that exclusion, so it is defended rather than accidental** — an exclusion nobody can red is indistinguishable from a selector that was never tested. 🗂️ **THE 465 EXISTING ROWS ARE DELIBERATELY NOT DELETED (RETENTION LAW: untracked factory history is never erased, and F-2398-2 forbids TRACKING this file because `run-guards.mjs:307–320` appends only while it is untracked — `git add`-ing it permanently ends the collection).** ➡️ **THEREFORE, FOR THE NEXT READER: every row at or before `2026-09-01` is fixture output and must be discarded; the prior starts accumulating REAL rows from the next gate run after this commit. The `seconds: 0` test is the durable discriminator — no real leg is instantaneous.** ⓘ **AND ONE INCIDENTAL DATUM THAT BEARS DIRECTLY ON THE OWNER'S LIVE FORK: rooting this guard edits `package.json`, which is in `ENGINE_SOURCE_INPUTS`, so THIS FIRE MOVED THE ENGINE IDENTITY HASH — by exactly the `test:*` chain edit F-1300-4 MANDATES. That is F-2417-1's 70% rate happening once more in real time, and a live instance for F-2416-2(ii).** The board's engine-era-guard was already red before and remains red after; nothing about that state changed. 💡 **REUSABLE: when a tool ANCHORS its output path to its own script tree — the correct cure for cwd-dependence — it becomes unsafe for any TEST that spawns the real binary, because the test can move `cwd` and cannot move the anchor. Ask of every anchored writer: who spawns this in a test, and where do its WRITES land? And when a persisted corpus finally gets its first reader, validate the corpus before believing a single number in it — a history assembled from fixtures looks exactly like a history, right down to the commit shas.** Evidence: `scripts/run-guards.mjs:45-46,307-320` (anchor + append), `scripts/run-guards.test.mjs:9,33-45` (real-script resolution + the cure), `scripts/guard-stats-persistence.test.mjs:27` (the sibling that always redirected), `logs/guard-stats.jsonl` (465 rows, all `seconds: 0`), `scripts/guard-stats-test-isolation-guard.test.mjs` (guard). Prior: F-2312-1 ⑦(b), F-2309-1, F-2398-2, F-2220-1, F-2217-1, F-2215-1, F-2166-2.
280:📊 **F-2417-1 — THE ENGINE IDENTITY HASH IS WRONG ABOUT WHAT IT CLAIMS TO MEASURE 70% OF THE TIME IT MOVES. MEASURED, NOT ARGUED (s2417, 2026-09-01).** ⚖️ **THIS ROW RENDERS NO RULING AND MUST NOT BE READ AS ONE.** F-2416-2 puts a two-part question on the owner's desk, and its part (ii) — *should `package.json` be in `ENGINE_SOURCE_INPUTS` at all, when F-1300-4 REQUIRES every ledger-writing fire to edit it?* — has been argued three times from ANECDOTE: F-2408-1 had **one** commit, s2416 had **two of three**. A design fork deserves a RATE, and a rate is a fire's call in a way the fork is not. 📊 **CENSUSED ACROSS THE WHOLE ENFORCEABLE ERA WINDOW (`d67608bb3`, the 2026-08-25 era-3 enforceability gate probe, → HEAD): 548 first-parent commits, of which 40 MOVED THE ENGINE IDENTITY CORPUS. Of those 40 — BOOKKEEPING-ONLY 28 (70.0%) · SUBSTANTIVE 12 (30.0%). And the 28 are not a mixed bag: in 28 OF 28 the ENTIRE `package.json` delta is a `test:*` chain, i.e. exactly and only the edit F-1300-4 mandates.** Not one of the 28 touched a file the headless replay executes. 🔑 **SO THE FACTORY'S OWN LAW IS THE MAJORITY AUTHOR OF ITS ENGINE'S IDENTITY CHANGES** — the corpus does not merely *admit* a non-behavioural input, it is DOMINATED by one, and the ratio is stable rather than a recent spike (the 28 run from 2026-08-25 to 2026-09-01, roughly one every other fire-day). 🧪 **THE KEY WAS VALIDATED AGAINST A DEFECT ALREADY PROVEN BY HAND BEFORE ANY NUMBER WAS BELIEVED (F-2215-1's rule, and the `validate-a-census-key-against-a-defect-you-know-existed` duty): F-2408-1 bisected the corpus by hand and named `daae36dc7` as a package.json-only guard-rooting movement — this census independently classes it `BOOKKEEPING (test-chain only)`; and the known-substantive control `a73790d9a` (embodied-build-v2, `src/systems/BuildSystem.ts`) classes `SUBSTANTIVE`. It also REPRODUCES s2416's hand count EXACTLY by a different method: since the era-5 pin at `c5bc0245d` there are 3 corpus movements — `daae36dc7` BOOKKEEPING · `b80253bff` SUBSTANTIVE (`src/playbook/PlaybookFormat.ts`) · `50a661cf3` BOOKKEEPING — i.e. "two of the three", arrived at independently.** ⓘ **TWO METHOD NOTES, because either one silently shrinks the numerator: (1) the corpus is READ FROM THE CODE at HEAD (`ENGINE_SOURCE_INPUTS`, 11 entries) and never transcribed, so a corpus edit cannot leave the census measuring yesterday's question; (2) `git log --name-only` is BLIND TO MERGE COMMITS, and 12 of these 40 are merges — the census uses `git show --first-parent --name-only` and the control asserts merges really do yield names (`0d718cead` 6 · `a73790d9a` 14 · `c5bc0245d` 18). A merge-blind census would have reported roughly two-thirds of the movements and every substantive drain would have vanished from it, inverting the headline.** 🔴 **AND THE LIVE STATE WAS RE-DERIVED AGAINST MAIN RATHER THAN INHERITED, exactly as s2416 instructed: `computeEngineHash(main)` → `417ac150…c3c864`; era 5 pins exactly one hash, `c0a015ae…`; `MATCH: false`. `scripts/engine-era-guard.test.mjs` is the FIRST leg of `test:node-guards` and it is RED ON MAIN RIGHT NOW — measured this fire, not assumed: rc=1, `engine hash changed from c0a015ae… to 417ac150…; append a same-era pin with its cause, or bump the era`.** ⓘ Stated honestly: I ran that LEG (149 ms), not the ~530 s battery, so the claim is about the leg and about the battery's exit code, which one red leg decides. 🚫 **NO GUARD IS PROPOSED, AND THE RESTRAINT IS THE FINDING RESTATED: a red on "the corpus moved for bookkeeping" would fire on 70% of the movements — i.e. during ordinary, correct, law-mandated fire work — and would be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate). That is precisely what F-2408-1 watched begin on its first day when the lane runner dismissed this very red as "unrelated current-main failures". A guard cannot fix a corpus definition; only the owner's ruling on F-2416-2(ii) can.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is the `identity-corpus-can-include-a-file-your-own-law-mandates-editing` lesson with a number attached: when an identity hash keeps moving for reasons nobody believes are identity changes, do not ask whether the corpus is DEFENSIBLE — measure what FRACTION of its movements the thing it claims to identify actually caused. A corpus that is right 30% of the time is not a strict instrument, it is a coin that reds.** GATE: owner ruling on F-2416-2 — this row supplies its missing input and does not pre-empt it.
